{
 "A travel assistant answering in English and Swahili writes one word per pass of its big model. A cheap draft can guess several words ahead, and the big model checks them all in one pass.": "1d3a2eb8b1.mp3",
 "EAGLE drops the separate small model. A tiny head reads the big model's own hidden states, so its guesses agree with the big model far more often.": "602e9032dc.mp3",
 "Each guess is kept with a probability set by how much the big model likes it. On the first miss, the replacement comes from what is left over. The answers come out exactly as the big model alone would give them.": "a9971cf89b.mp3",
 "Train the head only on the right answers, and it never learns to recover from its own wrong guesses. Long drafts fall apart, and the speed-up shrinks.": "1b70671f87.mp3",
 "Replace a rejected word by sampling the big model's whole distribution, and some words come out too often. It is faster, but no longer the same model.": "7a3735f5f6.mp3",
 "The check writes every guess into the memory cache. Forget to cut the rejected ones back out, and they leak into the answer.": "1fe4435296.mp3"
}
